+// The fake-socket backend is the single in-process transport of the embedded
+// (MOBILEAPP) server: SocketPoll wakeup pipes, kit connections and the accept
+// poll all share theMutex/theCV/fds. Those objects are translation-unit
+// statics, so exit-time static destruction tears them down in an order that is
+// unrelated to the threads still using them: SocketPoll::joinThread -> stop ->
+// wakeup -> fakeSocketWrite and a finishing pollingThreadEntry -> removeSockets
+// -> Socket::closeFD -> fakeSocketClose all lock theMutex. If the mutex was
+// already destroyed, pthread_mutex_lock returns EINVAL, std::mutex::lock throws
+// std::system_error, and because the throw escapes a noexcept destructor the
+// process aborts (observed at XCTest/NSApplication exit: COOLWSDServer dtor ->
+// stop -> joinThread, plus a concurrent polling thread on the same mutex).
+// net/Socket.cpp already keeps its cross-translation-unit wakeup state alive
+// for exactly this reason (getPollWakeupsMutex/getWakeupsArray are heap
+// objects that are intentionally never destroyed). Give the whole shared
+// backend the same process lifetime instead of relying on an unspecified
+// cross-TU destruction order; the leak is bounded and ends with the process.
+static std::mutex& theMutex = *new std::mutex;
+static std::condition_variable& theCV = *new std::condition_variable;
 
 static int fakeSocketLogLevel = -1;
 
 static void fakeSocketDumpStateImpl();
 
